-- Column kind from the first letter: lowercase makes
-- a SYM, uppercase a NUM.
function Col(name,at)
  return (name:find"^%l" and Sym or Num)(name,at) end -- #:noir

function Num(name,at)
  name = name or ""
  return new(NUM, {at=at or 1, name=name, n=0, mu=0, m2=0,
                   heaven = name:find"-$" and 0 or 1}) end -- #:heaven

function Sym(name,at)
  return new(SYM, {at=at or 1, name=name or "", n=0, has={}}) end

function SYM.add(i,v,inc)
  if v == "?" then return v end -- #:missing
  inc = inc or 1
  i.n = i.n + inc
  i.has[v] = inc + (i.has[v] or 0)
  if i.has[v] <= 0 then i.has[v] = nil end
  return v end

function NUM.add(i,v,inc,    d) -- #:protocol
  if v == "?" then return v end
  inc  = inc or 1
  i.n  = i.n + inc
  d    = v - i.mu
  i.mu = i.mu + inc * d / i.n -- #:welford
  i.m2 = i.m2 + inc * d * (v - i.mu); return v end -- #:stream

:noir noir
Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio (Stevens 1946): the four
scales of measurement, collapsed here to two -- symbols you can
only count (nominal) and numbers you can subtract. One header
letter decides which: lowercase first letter makes a Sym,
uppercase a Num. The header row is the entire schema: policy as
one line of data, mechanism in the code.

:heaven heaven
A goal's best normalized value: 0 for a minimized goal (`Lbs-`),
1 for a maximized one (`Mpg+`). `disty` is each row's distance
to heaven; the tree's best mark is the row nearest it.

Careful: flip this one token (`and 1 or 0`) and nothing crashes,
every test passes -- and the optimizer hunts the heaviest,
thirstiest car. Goal-encoding bugs steer perfect machinery
toward the wrong objective; only eyeballing output catches them.

:missing missing values
`"?"` means missing, and it is ignored on the way in -- the
summary only ever learns from what was seen. In this course's
data most goal cells start as "?", since labels are the thing we
cannot afford.

:protocol columnProtocol
Num and Sym answer the same eight questions -- add, sub, mid,
div, norm, dist, holds, reset -- one polymorphic protocol, two
implementations (mid is mean or mode; div is sd or entropy).
Everything downstream (tables, distance, trees) talks to the
protocol, never to the type. Duck typing with a contract.

:welford welford
Welford's 1962 one-pass update: mean and variance from a stream,
no stored data, no catastrophic cancellation. After each value v:

    n' = n+1
    d  = v - mu
    mu'= mu + d/n'
    m2'= m2 + d*(v - mu')

then sd = sqrt(m2/(n-1)). These four lines are exactly the code
at left.

:stream stream (and forgetting)
A summary you can update -- and un-update -- one datum at a
time, in constant memory. Run with `inc=-1` the Welford algebra
inverts: subtract a value and the stats roll back. That is why a
table can forget rows as cheaply as it learned them, and why
`(a+b)-b == a` is a testable law (`--without`, `--sub` in
ezr-eg1).
